 groups in romania and  bulgaria so that really is deterrence by  denial which we've done there should  russia invade you will go to a different  posture which is  deterrence by punishment and that  includes sanctions  what about this idea of de-escalation  russia again saying that it's removing  tanks for example uh overnight uh some  tank withdrawal um the irish um the  russian ambassador to ireland get it  right uh quoted by the task news agency  has suggested that the russian forces in  the west will be back to normal in three  or four weeks um another saying here of  course is we wait to see if it really  happens but are you getting a sense that  actually when these exercises are over  that indeed might be the case  no i don't think so yet but uh rather  bizarrely it was stalin who said trust  people but check and that checking the  verification by surveillance will tell  you this so really it's the surveillance  flights and the satellite imagery which  will tell you if in uh for space and  time the russians still have a fight  tonight capability there and it's not  really seeing a few tanks in crimea sort  of going from a to b it's really seeing  an off-ramp of combined arms army  capabilities long-range fires or  helicopters returning to the base  further into  russian territory which will give you an  indication because it's not battalion  tactical groups which will be  significant if there's any uh war it  would be those combined arms army  capabilities and really it would be for  me to see capabilities withdrawing from  belarus rather than ukraine because from  my perspective i think the central  gravity the source of all the strength  of the ukrainians is the resistance  around kiev i don't think the russians  would achieve any strategic objectives  just by going into the donbass or the  crimea it would actually reinforce the  fact that ukraine would still be  westward-looking after any invasion and  that's really the dilemma the russians  have which real