d do is there would be  embargoed from u.s technology and the  third one which is the variable again  which we really talked about a bit  earlier is that we would arm  the ukrainians for the war after the war  assuming there is one if if putin cannot  have an early victory you have a war  after the war which raises political and  economic costs to him finally you've got  this difficult one about nordstrom 2 and  i say it's difficult there's been some  fairly poor analysis on this so there's  actually a mutual dependency on the gas  from oil  73  of russia's gas goes to the eu  they don't want to lose that income now  we can we can uh we can sustain not  having russian gas for a number of  months and of course we also will have  been gaining the fact that we might be  able to have supply from somewhere else  but that's a another key variable both  for the west and the east but an eu  stress test in 2014 said that we could  uh outlast for a number of months anyway  uh any problems with gas being turned  off from russia that doesn't mean that  there's not a different issue about the  cost of gas but it's not the supply  which is in the short term an issue and  if we're in the issue of a long term  term war then we've got a different  issue for russia anyway the first thing  we would say is that armies don't fight  wars nations fight wars and what putin  needs to achieve is the dislocation of  both the armed forces of ukraine which i  think he could do in the short term the  dislocation of the political entity of  ukraine i.e needs to go to kiev and put  in a puppet regime and thirdly he needs  to dislocate the ukrainian people those  45 percent who at least have said that  they will sign up to any resistance  against putin now what he's really done  since april last year when we had the  first war scare  is try and uh  impose some psychological pressure on  the ukrainians and actually i think uh  president zielinski has been very good  at making sure that alert fatigue